  usage: * This suffix is, in most cases, only reserved for casual conversation and while it is a variant of -ear it's still productive on its own, specially in informal speech. However, when translated into writing, most of the verbs using it are written with -ear instead of -iar. Cf. webear and webiar or luquear and luquiar.\n* In most countries it's considered very informal, especially in writing.
  etymology: From -ear, following an alteration of -e- into -i- derived from a colloquial synaeresis.
  gloss: alternative form of "-ear"
