Sangley
pos: adj
  meta: {{es-adj|f=Sangleya}}
  etymology: From Hokkien, possibly either:\n* 常來, as labeled in the Boxer Codex (1590) and explained in the Bocabulario de la lengua sangleya por las letraz de el A.B.C. (1617) as per Van der Loon (1967), from which Mandarin "常來人" referring to Sangleys originates and consistent with Francisco de Sande (1576)'s description of "Throughout these islands they call the Chinese 'Sangleyes', meaning 'a  people who come and go,' on account of their habit of coming annually to these islands to trade, or, as they say there, 'the regular port'." as per Adelung (1817), Manuel (1948), and Go (2014-2015)\n* 商來, as explained in the Bocabulario de la lengua sangleya por las letraz de el A.B.C. (1617) as per Van der Loon (1967)\n* 生理 (“business; livelihood”) as in 生理人 (“merchant; tradesman; trafficker”), according to Go (2014-2015) and  Manuel (1948) as "sieng-lí" recorded in Piñol (1937)\n* 商旅 (“travelling merchant”), according to Manuel (1948) & Hofileña (2011)\n* 送來 or 生理人來, according to Chan-Yap (1980). \n* 誰來, as per Norberto Romualdez in Manuel (1948) who says that "sangley is a Spanish corruption of sang lay, signifying "who comes", an expression used by the Chinese in the Islands to greet a ship coming from China."\nAccording to Quilis et al. (1997), Wenceslao Retana (1921) commented before (sic):\n\n"(del chino xiang-lay, mercader.) adj. Nombre que en lo antiguo se dio en Filipinas a los mercaderes chinos, y que luego se hizo genérico de los de esta raza residentes en aquellas islas"\nVan der Loon (1967) additionally surmises that perhaps it is not of Chinese origin.\n\nCompare Tagalog "Sanglay", Ilocano "Sanglay", etc.
  gloss: alternative form of "sangley" (“Chinese (historically pertaining to traders)”)
    q: Philippines, historical
    syn: chino
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m}}
  g: m
  etymology: From Hokkien, possibly either:\n* 常來, as labeled in the Boxer Codex (1590) and explained in the Bocabulario de la lengua sangleya por las letraz de el A.B.C. (1617) as per Van der Loon (1967), from which Mandarin "常來人" referring to Sangleys originates and consistent with Francisco de Sande (1576)'s description of "Throughout these islands they call the Chinese 'Sangleyes', meaning 'a  people who come and go,' on account of their habit of coming annually to these islands to trade, or, as they say there, 'the regular port'." as per Adelung (1817), Manuel (1948), and Go (2014-2015)\n* 商來, as explained in the Bocabulario de la lengua sangleya por las letraz de el A.B.C. (1617) as per Van der Loon (1967)\n* 生理 (“business; livelihood”) as in 生理人 (“merchant; tradesman; trafficker”), according to Go (2014-2015) and  Manuel (1948) as "sieng-lí" recorded in Piñol (1937)\n* 商旅 (“travelling merchant”), according to Manuel (1948) & Hofileña (2011)\n* 送來 or 生理人來, according to Chan-Yap (1980). \n* 誰來, as per Norberto Romualdez in Manuel (1948) who says that "sangley is a Spanish corruption of sang lay, signifying "who comes", an expression used by the Chinese in the Islands to greet a ship coming from China."\nAccording to Quilis et al. (1997), Wenceslao Retana (1921) commented before (sic):\n\n"(del chino xiang-lay, mercader.) adj. Nombre que en lo antiguo se dio en Filipinas a los mercaderes chinos, y que luego se hizo genérico de los de esta raza residentes en aquellas islas"\nVan der Loon (1967) additionally surmises that perhaps it is not of Chinese origin.\n\nCompare Tagalog "Sanglay", Ilocano "Sanglay", etc.
  gloss: alternative form of "sangley" (“ethnic Chinese person (person of pure ethnic Chinese ancestry, especially during the Spanish colonial period in the Philippines)”)
    q: Philippines, historical
    syn: chino
  gloss: alternative form of "sangley" (“ethnic Chinese merchant”)
    q: Philippines, historical, archaic
