agua
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|f}}
  g: f
  usage: * The feminine noun agua is like other feminine nouns starting with a stressed a sound in that it takes the definite article el (normally reserved for masculine nouns) in the singular when there is no intervening adjective:\n:: el agua\n* However, if an adjective, even one that begins with a stressed a sound such as alta or ancha, intervenes between the article and the noun, the article reverts to la.\n* In Guatemala, since agua prototypically refers to a soft drink, the phrase agua pura is used to refer to water.
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "agua", from Latin "aqua" (compare Catalan "aigua", French "eau", Galician "auga", Italian "acqua", Occitan "aiga", Portuguese "água", Romanian "apă", Sardinian "abba"), from Proto-Indo-European "*h₂ekʷeh₂". See English "ea".
  gloss: water
  gloss: body of water
    syn: cuerpo de agua
  gloss: (meteorology) rain (Condensed water falling from a cloud.)
    syn: lluvia; aguacero
  gloss: river; stream
