alcanzar
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}}
  usage: When alcanzar is used to mean to peak or to peak at (sense 5), it is usually followed by some measurable direct object like "el puesto", "el número", "un punto máximo". Thus, alcanzar in this case means to reach. Ultimately, the way to say that someone "peaked" in their career or a song "peaked" on the charts is expressed as "to reach a high point of" or "to reach the position of".
  etymology: From Vulgar Latin "*incalciāre" (ultimately related to Latin "calx" (“heel”)), influenced by Latin "ad" and/or the Spanish preposition al. Compare the form encalzar, Portuguese "encalçar", Catalan "encalçar". Also compare English "causeway", cause- being derived from calciare (“to tread”), which Spanish "calzar" also descends from.
  gloss: to reach; to get to to catch up
    q: transitive
    syn: percanzar
  gloss: to accomplish; to achieve
    q: transitive
  gloss: to be enough; to suffice
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to pass
    q: transitive, especially Latin America
  gloss: to peak, to peak at
    q: transitive
    syn: llegar
  gloss: to get it (to receive punishment; to receive a beating)
    q: transitive, Canary
