andar
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}} {{es-conj|andarse}}
  usage: * In Spanish and many other Romance languages, this verb originally only meant "to walk", but also later acquired the secondary sense of "being" (temporarily). In many cases, the verb can be used interchangeably with the principal verb for "to be (temporarily)" - in Spanish, andar can be used interchangeably with estar in informal situations, although estar is generally more widely used.\n* Usage of the regular root and- for the preterite (andé, andaste, etc.), the imperfect subjunctive (andara/andase, andaras/andases, etc.), and the future subjunctive, is proscribed but more common in Spain. Many speakers in Spain consider the forms with -uv- literary or poetic. The forms with -uv- dominate in Latin America, although the regular forms are still occasionally seen.
  etymology: Inherited from Early Medieval Latin andāre.\n\nThe preterite's origin is unclear, most likely generalized from the preterite of haber (“to have”), hub- (note that b and v are pronounced identically; compare the same development in tener, and more alike to this verb, estar).
  gloss: to walk, to go
    syn: caminar; ir
  gloss: to amble, to travel
  gloss: to ride
  gloss: to function, to work, to run, to go
    syn: funcionar; marchar
  gloss: to pass, to go by, to elapse
    syn: pasar
  gloss: to go about; to busy oneself with
  gloss: to be, to feel
    syn: estar
  gloss: to go around, to move or spread from person to person
  gloss: to go round (doing something) (describing interrupted, frequent activity) [+gerund (doing something)]
    q: catenative
  gloss: to have been feeling; to feel emotions over a period of time
    q: copulative
  gloss: to go out with, to date [+con (someone)]
    syn: salir
  gloss: to have or carry with oneself, to go around with (money, a vehicle)
    q: Central America
  gloss: to go away, to leave
    q: reflexive
    syn: irse; marcharse; largarse
  gloss: to take out, to remove oneself
    q: reflexive, imperative
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m}}
  g: m
  etymology: Inherited from Early Medieval Latin andāre.\n\nThe preterite's origin is unclear, most likely generalized from the preterite of haber (“to have”), hub- (note that b and v are pronounced identically; compare the same development in tener, and more alike to this verb, estar).
  gloss: walk, pace, gait
    syn: paseo; zancada
  gloss: behavior, manner
    syn: comportamiento; conducta; manera
