arañar
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}}
  etymology: Uncertain. Possibly derived from arar (“to plough”), from Latin "arāre", with the sense taken from that of making shallow furrows or grooves. Alternatively, linked to araña (“spider”) from Latin "arānea", though a semantic link is difficult; perhaps the meaning stemmed from the spider being an aggressive animal, to that of fighting or quarreling, and later a secondary sense of "scratch" developed as a result (compare Old French "hargner" (“to fight”) and modern French "hargneux" (“aggressive”), which may be of Germanic origin instead; see also old Old Italian "aragnarsi" (“to come to blows”)). It may instead have a link to the meaning developed in Romanian "râie" (“scabies, mange, itch”), which itself was derived from the same Latin word, arānea (cf. also Spanish "roña" and Italian "rogna" (“scabies, mange”), which may come from a variant form *aronea; compare aruñar, the alternative form of arañar, which may alternatively have been influenced by uña (“nail”)). In this theory, the original meaning was to scrape or scratch oneself to relieve an itch, but this has not been attested. It is possible the relation with arānea is more indirect, however.
  gloss: to scratch
    q: transitive
    syn: rascar; rasguñar
_____
arañazo
