arrecir
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb|<no_pres_stressed>}} {{es-conj|<no_pres_stressed>|nocomb=1}}
  usage: Used only with forms where the ending begins with -i.
  etymology: From Vulgar Latin "arrigēscĕre" (“to become stiff”), from Latin "arrigĕre" (“raise; rouse, excite”). According to Coromines and Pascual, this infinitive arrecir "barely exists" (apenas existe), and prefer to link the adjective arrecido to a supposed verb *arreeçer, of the same etymology, cognate with Galician "arrecerse" (“get a common cold; become numb”).
  gloss: to make something/someone numb with cold
    q: transitive, uncommon
  gloss: to become numbed by cold, to be numbed by cold
    q: reflexive
