arrufar
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}}
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "arrufar", of uncertain origin. First attested c. 1250 in the sense "to become conceited".\n\nSaid originally to have been borrowed from a gem language, compare ruffle or hrufla. However, it is difficult for a word mostly attested in northern Europe to travel down all the way to Iberia, with the West Germanic examples possibly being borrowed from Romance languages as well. It could be derived from Latin "rūfus" (“red-haired”) as a derogatory term or from the custom of Roman prostitutes to dress themselves with blond wigs, or in another way, from Latin "rūfula" (“prostitute”) via Vulgar Latin "*rūfulanus". Compare with Galician "arrufar", Portuguese "arrufar" and Italian "ruffiano".
  gloss: to incite; to provoke
    q: transitive
  gloss: to build a sheer (curve of deck) in a ship
    q: ambitransitive
  gloss: to become vain or conceited
    q: obsolete, pronominal, intransitive
  gloss: to snarl; to growl
    q: obsolete, pronominal, intransitive
