pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}}
  usage: In Latin America, beber tends to be used more with the meaning of "to drink alcohol", and tomar tends to be used instead for the normal "drink" sense. In Spain, both beber and tomar are common for the "drink" sense, with beber being the default verb. Beber is also commonly used in Spain in the "drink alcohol" sense.
  etymology: From Old Spanish "bever", inherited from Latin "bibere". Cognate with Catalan "beure", French "boire", Italian "bere", Portuguese "beber", Romanian "bea". The modern spelling with intervocalic <b> replacing medieval <v> is due to Latin influence.
  gloss: to drink
    q: transitive, intransitive, sometimes reflexive
    syn: tomar
  gloss: to drink alcohol
    q: transitive, intransitive
  gloss: to draw from (to be inspired by)
    q: transitive, figurative
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m}}
  g: m
  etymology: From Old Spanish "bever", inherited from Latin "bibere". Cognate with Catalan "beure", French "boire", Italian "bere", Portuguese "beber", Romanian "bea". The modern spelling with intervocalic <b> replacing medieval <v> is due to Latin influence.
  gloss: drinking
