cantúo
pos: adj
  meta: {{es-adj}}
  usage: In Andalusian speech, while the masculine cantúo is universally understood as a compliment regarding a strong or handsome physique, the feminine cantúa can sometimes lean slightly towards a vulgar or hyper-sexualized catcall depending on the tone and context.
  etymology: From canto (“edge, thickness, chunk”) + the suffix -udo (denoting abundance or prominence), with standard Andalusian dropping of the intervocalic -d- to yield -úo. Literally "having prominent edges or thickness".
  gloss: Well-built, strapping, robust, hefty or physically attractive and thickset. (Usually referring to someone with a strong, muscular, or shapely physique)
    q: Andalusia, colloquial
    syn: fuerte; troncho; (colloquial) petao; (slang) buenorro
  gloss: Substantial, hefty, large, thick
    q: Andalusia, colloquial, of an object or abstraction
    syn: grueso; gordo; hermoso
