catalufo
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m|f=+}}
  g: m
  etymology: Probably a blend of catalán ("Catalan") + Catalan "castellufo" ("a speaker of Spanish"). The second element, castellufo, is a Catalan word made of castellufa, a derogatory name for the inhabitants of Es Castell by the Spanish-speaking military; itself made of castellà (“a Spanish speaker”) and llufa (“a silent fart”), meaning that a term with the opposite meaning was created from it. Another possibility is that it is simply a blend of catalán ("Catalan") + pitufo ("smurf") for stereotypical reasons, comparing the Smurfs’ village to any typical Catalan village, the Smurfs’ caps to the barretina, and the stereotypical notion that Catalans are small-town folk. It could also simply come from catalufa.
  gloss: Catalan secessionist
    q: slang, derogatory, Spanish politics
    syn: polaco; lazi; separrata; catalino
