  etymology: According to the Real Academia Española, from Caló "čingarár" (“to fight”), from Romani "ćingardel" (“to fight, to have an argument”), likely from Ashokan Prakrit "*𑀘𑀺𑀗𑁆𑀖𑀸𑀝", *𑀘𑀺𑀗𑁆𑀖𑀸𑀭 (“noise, scream”), probably of onomatopoeic origin similar to that of Sanskrit "चीत्कार" (“cries, noise”), with semantic shift "noise, scream" > "row, quarrel" > "brawl, fight" > "screw, bother". Compare also Hindi "चिंघाड़" (“shriek, roar”).
  gloss: to bother, to fuck with
    q: Mx, vulgar
  gloss: to engage in sexual intercourse, to fuck
    q: Mx, US, vulgar, literally
    syn: coger; joder; follar; copular
  gloss: to be wrong; to screw up, to fuck up
    q: Mx, vulgar
  gloss: to steal, to swipe
    q: Mx, vulgar
  gloss: to break
    q: Ch, Spain, Gu, Mx, US, vulgar
  gloss: to fail, flop, lose
    q: Ch, Spain, Gu, Mx, US, intransitive, vulgar
  gloss: to work hard
    q: Spain, Gu, Ho, Mx, US, reflexive, vulgar
  gloss: to eat or drink
    q: Mx, US, vulgar
  gloss: to splash
    q: Canary
    syn: salpicar
