cien
pos: num
  usage: *The word cien is an apocopation of ciento. These two forms are generally not interchangeable; their use can be determined by the following rules:\n** cien is used:\n*** when not followed by other numerals: i.e., with the number "100" itself and numbers that end in "-100" in their numerical form (both as a quantity and as a standalone numeral):\n***: {{coi|es|cien personas|one hundred people}}\n***: {{coi|es|dos mil cien personas|two thousand one hundred people}}\n***: {{coi|es|contar hasta el cien|to count up to one hundred}}\n***: noventa y ocho... noventa y nueve... ¡cien! ― ninety-eight... ninety-nine... one hundred!\n*** when immediately followed by a higher numeral to indicate a number with higher order of magnitude:\n***: {{coi|es|cien mil|one hundred thousand}}\n***: {{coi|es|cien mil uno|one hundred thousand and one}}\n***: {{coi|es|cien millones|one hundred million}}\n** ciento is used:\n*** when immediately followed by a lower numeral to indicate a number with more significant figures:\n***: {{coi|es|ciento dos|one hundred and two}}\n***: {{coi|es|ciento cincuenta y ocho|one hundred and fifty-eight}}\n***: {{coi|es|ciento cincuenta y ocho mil|one hundred and fifty-eight thousand}}\n*** To indicate percentages:\n***: {{coi|es|cincuenta y uno por ciento|fifty-one per cent}}\n**** A notable exception to this is the percentage 100%, which allows the use of either cien or ciento. In particular, cien por cien, ciento por ciento and cien por ciento (but not *ciento por cien) are all considered valid by the Royal Spanish Academy. Spain chiefly uses cien por cien, while most of Latin America prefers ciento por ciento.\n**Set phrases, idioms and proverbs may use either cien or ciento, depending on the phrase: de todo a cien; poner a cien; ciento y la madre; de ciento en viento; quien hace un cesto hará ciento, si le dan mimbres y tiempo.\n* Each form has a corresponding plural – respectively cienes and cientos – which are likewise not interchangeable. In particular:\n** When referring to a quantity, whether arbitrary or groups of 100 units, the standard way is to use cientos:\n**: {{coi|es|cientos de dólares|hundreds of dollars}}\n**: {{coi|es|dos cientos de manzanas|two sets of one hundred apples}} (note that this is not the same as "two hundred apples", which is doscientas manzanas – see below)\n*:The use of cienes in these contexts can be found in some countries of Central America, but the Royal Spanish Academy discourages such use.\n** In standard Spanish, cienes is effectively reserved only when referring to the figure 100 itself: \n**: Hay dos cienes en esta hoja de papel. ― There are two hundreds (= occurrences of the number 100) on this piece of paper.\n** Numerals that are multiples of 100 have their own forms: doscientos (200), trescientos (300), cuatrocientos (400), quinientos (500), seiscientos (600), setecientos (700), ochocientos (800) and novecientos (900). Unlike cien and ciento, which are not gendered, these numerals change depending on the gender of the objects: their respective feminine forms end in -as instead of -os (doscientas, trescientas etc.).
  etymology: From ciento, from Latin "centum".
  gloss: one hundred (100)
