  meta: {{es-noun|m}}
  g: m
  usage: Unlike the English term borrowed from it, the Spanish term refers to both the plants and the seeds.
  etymology: Uncertain; possibly from an unattested regional Vulgar Latin variant of Latin "coriandrum"—where also its doublet culantro, today obsolete, comes from—with a front vowel, like *ciliandrum or *ceriandrum. (Compare French "coriandre", Italian "coriandolo", Portuguese "coentro") from Ancient Greek "κορίανδρον", alternative form of κορίαννον (“coriander”).)
  gloss: coriander, cilantro
