cobre
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m|-}}
  g: m
  etymology: Inherited from Late Latin "cuprum" (“copper”), from Latin "cyprium (aes)" (“Cypriot copper”), from Ancient Greek "Κύπρος", because Cyprus was its chief source. The irregular evolution of this term in Spanish and other Ibero-Romance languages like Portuguese "cobre" (with the expected result being *cobro) has been suggested to perhaps be due to some influence from Catalan "coure", although this is uncertain because of the discrepancy between the internal consonants and the unusual idea of just altering the final consonants due to foreign influence. More likely, it may have been informally influenced by alambre (older arambre), originally meaning bronze in Old Spanish.
  gloss: copper (reddish-brown, malleable, ductile metallic element with high electrical and thermal conductivity, symbol Cu, and atomic number 29)
_____
