cocer
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}}
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "cozer", from Late Latin "cocere", from Latin "coquere". Cognate with English "cook".\n\nSome conjugated forms of the verb were reformed through analogy with the infinitive; in older Spanish, the forms cueza and cuezo were cuega and cuego (< Late Latin "cocam", cocō), cocí was coxe (< Latin "coxī"), and the past participle was cocho (< Latin "coctus").
  gloss: to boil
  gloss: to cook (only used in situations where the food being prepared is submitted to fire; such as through the processes of boiling, simmering or steaming anything; or baking bread slowly in an oven)
  gloss: to brew
    q: reflexive, figurative
  gloss: to chafe (get sore)
    q: pronominal
    syn: escocerse; escaldarse
