cuál
pos: determiner
  meta: {{es-det|pl=cuáles}}
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "qual", from Latin "quālis" (“which”).
  gloss: what; which
    q: chiefly Latin America
    syn: qué
pos: pron
  meta: {{es-pronoun|pl=cuáles}}
  usage: * Qué and cuál are used in interrogative sentences and should not be confused with que and cual.\n* Usually, qué and cuál are translated into English as what and which, respectively. There are a few exceptions to this general rule that often trip up learners.\n*: In questions with the verb ser (“to be”), qué is used to ask about definitions of things:\n*:: ¿Qué es un nombre? ― What is a name? (What does "name" mean?)\n*: If the question with ser is not about a definition, cuál is used:\n*:: ¿Cuál es tu nombre? ― What is your name?\n*: If qué was used here, the question would be understood to be about the literal definition of the name, like asking "What does your name mean?". More examples:\n*:: ¿Cuál es la capital de Francia? ― What is the capital of France?\n*:: ¿Cuál es el problema aquí? ― What is the problem here?\n*: Before a noun, qué and cuál are interchangeable and synonymous (except in exclamations, such as "¡Qué lástima!" (What a pity!), where only qué is used).\n*:: ¿Qué/cuál libro estás leyendo? ― What book are you reading?\n*:: ¿Qué/cuál respuesta crees que es la correcta? ― Which answer do you think is correct?\n*:: Dime a qué/cuáles países te gustaría viajar. ― Tell me which countries you would like to visit.\n:: In Spain, however, cuál is rarely used before a noun, and qué is typically used instead.
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "qual", from Latin "quālis" (“which”).
  gloss: (see usage notes) which one, what, which
_____
cuál es su nombre
pos: phrase
  meta: {{head|es|phrase}}
  etymology: cuál (“what”) + es (“is”) + su (“your”) + nombre (“name”).
  gloss: what is your name?
    q: formal
    syn: (informal) cómo te llamas; (informal) cuál es tu nombre
