deber
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}} {{es-conj|deberse|nocomb=1}}
  usage: * Usage in Spain differs from that of Latin America. Colloquially in Spain, deber in the sense of "must" (used to express obligation or requirement) is usually replaced with tener que, and deber is seen as formal in this context. However, it is commonly used in both regions to indicate probability or supposition (also translated as "must"). Deber is still used in Spain in the conditional and the preterite (with the meaning of should or should have), although a construction with deber in the conditional + haber + participle is more common than deber in the preterite (with an infinitive or haber + participle) in informal language. The sense of "owe" is used regularly in both regions.\n* The RAE (Royal Spanish Academy) differentiates between deber and deber de. According to their standard, deber should preferably be used to indicate an obligation or requirement, and deber de to express probability or supposition.\n* In the preterite, the infinitive construction and the construction with haber plus a participle are synonymous.\n* Conjugations such as debrá or debría are nonstandard.
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "dever", from Latin "dēbēre" (“owe”). Cognates include English "due" and debt.
  gloss: to owe
    q: ditransitive
  gloss: must, need to, have to (indicates an obligation or requirement) [(optionally) +de]
    q: auxiliary, not in the conditional, colloquially chiefly  Latam
    syn: (more common informally in Spain) tener que; (formal) haber de
  gloss: must (used to indicate that something is likely) [(optionally) +de]
    q: auxiliary, not in the conditional
    syn: tener que
  gloss: should (indicating opinion or expectation) [(optionally) +de]
    q: auxiliary, in the conditional
    _gloss: should have [(optionally) +de]
      q: auxiliary, in the preterite, colloquially chiefly  Latam
      syn: (more common informally in Spain) debería + haber + participle
    _gloss: should have [(optionally) +de]
      q: auxiliary, in the conditional + haber + participle
      syn: (both more formal in Spain) debí + infinitive / debí + haber + participle
  gloss: will need to [(optionally) +de]
    q: auxiliary, colloquially chiefly  Latam, in the future tense
    syn: tener que
  gloss: to be due to, to be attributable to, to be the result of, to be caused by
    q: reflexive  with preposition a
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m}}
  g: m
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "dever", from Latin "dēbēre" (“owe”). Cognates include English "due" and debt.
  gloss: obligation, duty
    syn: obligación
  gloss: debt
    syn: deuda
  gloss: should, ought (modal noun)
  gloss: homework
    q: Spain, usually in the plural
    syn: tarea
