esmayao
pos: adj
  meta: {{es-adj}}
  etymology: syncopic form of "esmayado". Cognate with English "dismay", Portuguese "desmaiar". Doublet of desmayar\n\nNo conjugated forms of the possible verb desmayarse 'to be hungry' have been found. Most likely from the same root as desmayar and dismay but without the -dis suffix.\n\n22-ii-1868 Jable (El Ómnibus, p.3) uses "estomagos desmayados" with the meaning of 'to be hungry', but later works differentiated between desmayar as 'faint' and esmayado as 'hungry'.\n\n"Esmayalse" also attested
  gloss: famished, hungry. Mainly used in southern Spanish dialects
    q: Spain, colloquial
_____
esmayar
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}}
  etymology: Borrowed from Old French "esmaier" (“to perturb”). Doublet of desmayar. Cognate with Galician "esmayar".\n\ndesmayar and esmayar used to be interchangeable, but esmayar is now obsolete and only found in its adjective form esmayado or more commonly esmayao (being hungry or famished), in southern Spanish dialects.\n\nNo conjugated instances of desmayar or esmayar meaning "being hungry or famished" have been found.\n\nThe sense of "being hungry" may have been influenced or derived from Galician-Portuguese esmagar "craving to eat".\n\nIt may have also developed by the fact that one faints when they are famished.
  gloss: to faint (lose consciousness)
    q: obsolete, pronominal
  gloss: to cause a person to faint
    q: obsolete, transitive
  gloss: to dishearten
    q: obsolete, intransitive
