estar
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}} {{es-conj|estarse|nocomb=1}}
  usage: See ser#Spanish. Contrast also with quedar. The usage of the second-person singular affirmative imperative must always be used pronominally.
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish estar, from Latin stō, stare.\n\n\nCompare English "stand". The preterite's origin is unclear, most likely generalized from the preterite of haber (“to have”), hub- (note that b and v are pronounced identically; compare the same development in andar and tener and Portuguese "estar"). Cognate with English "state". Preterite, imperfect subjunctive and future subjunctive forms are derived from tener.
  gloss: to be (have a temporary or permanent location in space)
    q: copulative, intransitive
  gloss: to be present
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to be (denotes a copula, in a transient fashion)
    q: copulative, intransitive
  gloss: to be (auxiliary verb for the progressive/continuous aspect, preceding the gerund of the verb)
    q: auxiliary
  gloss: to be in a state (in a passive voice sense)
    q: intransitive, auxiliary
  gloss: to be, stay (denotes a copula, in a transient fashion)
    q: reflexive, followed by adjective
  gloss: to be (still) [+por (+ infinitive) (to be done)]
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to be in a long-term state (in specific idioms)
    q: intransitive
