gabacho
pos: adj
  meta: {{es-adj}}
  etymology: Borrowed from Occitan "gavach" (“rustic; mountain-dweller, northerner, peasant”), which originally meant "bird's crop, goitre" and changed to a derogatory word due to the perceived higher incidence of diseases up north; compare Catalan "gavatx". It is also cognate with French "gabot,jabot", Servigliano caaciu, Neapolitan "gavattse", Piedmontese "gavagun" and Catalan "gavaig", all meaning "bird's crop" and French "gavion" (“throat”) and Picard "gave" (“stomach”) from a Vulgar Latin form *gauakte, of pre-Roman qfa-sub origin, possibly of cel origin.
  gloss: Pyrenean
    q: geography
  gloss: Gallic, frenchified
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m|f=+}}
  g: m
  etymology: Borrowed from Occitan "gavach" (“rustic; mountain-dweller, northerner, peasant”), which originally meant "bird's crop, goitre" and changed to a derogatory word due to the perceived higher incidence of diseases up north; compare Catalan "gavatx". It is also cognate with French "gabot,jabot", Servigliano caaciu, Neapolitan "gavattse", Piedmontese "gavagun" and Catalan "gavaig", all meaning "bird's crop" and French "gavion" (“throat”) and Picard "gave" (“stomach”) from a Vulgar Latin form *gauakte, of pre-Roman qfa-sub origin, possibly of cel origin.
  gloss: a villager from the Pyrenees
  gloss: a Frenchman, a frog, Frenchy, baguette
    q: colloquial, Spain, ethnic slur
    syn: franchute
  gloss: any white man (of any nation)
    q: colloquial, mildly pejorative, Texas
  gloss: foreigner, gringo, especially from the United States
    q: colloquial, derogatory, Mexico
