pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m}}
  g: m
  etymology: Inherited from Latin "gurguliō", variant of curculiō. Coromines and Pascual posit a Vulgar Latin "*gurgulius", back-derived from the Latin accusative gurguliōnem after speakers interpreted its ending as an augmentative (cf. Spanish -ón). They also acknowledge the possibility that gorgojo was simply inherited from the original Latin nominative. Compare Italian "gorgoglione", alongside the Old Italian "gorgoglio". For other Spanish animal names that appear to reflect the Latin nominative, cf. drago, pavo, and sierpe.
  gloss: grub
  gloss: weevil
    syn: curculiónido; picudo
  gloss: small person
    q: colloquial
