haber
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}} {{es-conj|haberse}}
  usage: * (to have) haber is no longer used with the sense of ownership, except in some idioms. The modern term to express ownership is tener (“to have”).\n* (there is/are) In standard usage, the verb is always conjugated in the third-person singular when used in this sense, even if the subject itself is plural:\n:: En esta tienda hay tres tipos de champú; en la otra solo hay uno. ― In this shop there are three types of shampoo; in the other one there is only one.\n:: El año pasado había ocho personas en mi fiesta de cumple; este año espero que haya al menos diez. ― Last year, there were eight people at my birthday party; this year, I expect there to be at least ten.\n* The first and second persons are rarely attested in the preterite; their respective forms are an extrapolation.\n* (past obligation) haber is used to make what is known as the imperativo retrospectivo, signifying a failed past obligation. It is used in the form infinitive + past participle, and only applies to the second person (singular or plural).
  etymology: Latinised respelling of Old Spanish "aver", from Latin "habēre, habeō" (“have, hold”), probably from Proto-Italic "*habēō" or *haβēō, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European "*gʰeh₁bʰ-" (“to grab, take”). Cognate with French "avoir", Italian "avere", Occitan "aver", Portuguese "haver", Romanian "avea", avere, and Sardinian (Campidanese "airi", Logudorese "àere").\n\nThe preterite is from metathesis of the Latin perfect habuī > *haubī.
  gloss: have; forms the perfect aspect [+masculine singular past participle]
    q: auxiliary
  gloss: to hold, to possess
    q: obsolete
  gloss: to exist; there be; “there is”, “there are” (hay); “there was”, “there were” (hubo​/​había)
    q: impersonal, in third person singular only
  gloss: to have to [+de (+ infinitive) (do something)]
    q: dated or formal
  gloss: to be necessary [+que (+ infinitive) (to do something)]
    q: used only in the third-person existential form
  gloss: used to denote a past obligation
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m}}
  g: m
  etymology: Latinised respelling of Old Spanish "aver", from Latin "habēre, habeō" (“have, hold”), probably from Proto-Italic "*habēō" or *haβēō, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European "*gʰeh₁bʰ-" (“to grab, take”). Cognate with French "avoir", Italian "avere", Occitan "aver", Portuguese "haver", Romanian "avea", avere, and Sardinian (Campidanese "airi", Logudorese "àere").\n\nThe preterite is from metathesis of the Latin perfect habuī > *haubī.
  gloss: asset
  gloss: history
  gloss: credit side
