halagar
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}}
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "falagar". Further Unknown: in 1884 the Royal Spanish Academy said from Arabic "حَلَاوَة" (“pleasing, agreeable thing”); in 1899 from Latin "flagitāre" (“to solicit”); in 1956 from Arabic "خَلَقَ" (“to make, forge, lie, polish, perfume”), which Coromines and Pascual support while mentioning a related خَالَقَ (“to treat someone kindly”); in 2001 from Andalusian Arabic "هَالَاق" (“thief pigeon”). Other possibilities: from Basque "palagau" (“to flatter, calm, give”), from Hebrew "הלל" (the -gar ending from Latin "-icare" as with cabalgar, comulgar, amargar; the last -l of the theme omitted for repetition, see idolatry).\n\nSee Galician "afagar", Asturian "afalagar", Basque "balakatu" (“to flatter”), Basque "palagukeria" (“flattery”), Basque "palagu" (“flattery, caress”), Basque "palaguka" (“caressing”), Aragonese "falaguera" (“impertinent extravagant desire, passion, swelter”), Catalan "afalagar" (“to stroke, flatter”), Catalan "falaguera" (“flattering, satisfying, contenting, appeasing, placating; light, quick: charm, charisma, grace, pull”).
  gloss: to make much of, to flatter, to entice
    q: transitive
    syn: adular
  gloss: to praise, to make much of
    q: transitive
    syn: alabar
  gloss: to caress, to please, to gratify, to soothe, to appease, to cocker
    q: transitive
    syn: acariciar; agradar; calmar; aliviar
