hambre
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|f}}
  g: f
  usage: * The feminine noun hambre is like other feminine nouns starting with a stressed a sound in that it takes the definite article el (normally reserved for masculine nouns) in the singular when there is no intervening adjective:\n:: el hambre\n* However, if an adjective, even one that begins with a stressed a sound such as alta or ancha, intervenes between the article and the noun, the article reverts to la.
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "fambre", fanbre, famne (whence also Ladino "ambre"), from Vulgar Latin "*faminem" (the accusative of a nominative *famen or *famis), variant of Latin "famēs", see there for more. For other examples of Latin /mVn/ > Old Spanish /mn/ > later /mbɾ/ see hombre, nombre, muchedumbre, cumbre, lumbre, alambre, hembra, costumbre, enjambre,  -dumbre, -umbre. Compare also Latin /mn/ > Old Spanish /ɲ/ ⟨mn > nn > ñ⟩ in dueño, sueño, daño, otoño, etc. Compare Portuguese "fome", Galician "fame", French "faim", dialectal Occitan "hame", Italian "fame", Sardinian "fámine", famen, Romanian "foame". Cognate with English "famine", famish. Doublet of fame.
  gloss: hunger
