herreruelo
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m}}
  g: m
  etymology: From older ferrehuelo, perhaps from Andalusian Arabic "فرويل", folk-etymologized into herrero ("blacksmith") + -uelo. Coromines appears confident in this interpretation of the Arabic word, though the usual sources on Andalusi Arabic do not mention it. He posits that the Andalusi term, in turn, derives from a 'vulgar' Maghrebi Arabic "فريول" later recorded in Rabat, Tanger and Tetouan as a cape used by certain sullied workers, itself from Latin "palliolum" (borrowed via la-afr).\n\nCompare Italian "ferraiolo" (presumably calqued from Spanish), Sicilian "firriolu", Maltese "firjol".
  gloss: a type of short cape without a hood (capilla or capucha)
    q: Spain
  gloss: a type of Spanish cavalry used in the mid- to- late 16th century wearing a cuirass or corselet and armed with wheellock pistols and either a spear or javelins
