_____
ir
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}} {{es-conj|irse}}
  usage: * ir is similar to estar in many senses.\n** Estar + gerund expresses the fact that the action of the verb is happening on the immediate present, with no inherent sense of past or future movement through time or space. It's the standard and more common expression.\n** "Ella está cantando" (She is singing) could imply: \n*** Current Action (She is singing right now)\n*** Continuous State (She is currently engaged in an event where she regularly sings, but might not be doing so at the moment)\n*** (now (dated) or (formal)): Habitual action "she sings in the morning every day"\n** ir + gerund expresses a kind of iterative or durative aspect. It emphasizes that the action involves movement or change over time, particularly along a longer process or journey, one that will usually be continued into the future. It is less common in casual speech and is mostly found in formal or literary works.\n** "Ella va cantando" (She goes on singing) could imply:\n*** She is moving and singing, either simultaneously or independently (She could move, stop to sing, and then continue moving). Either moving and singing with no defined direction through different locations (She goes around singing wherever she is) or along a specified path.\n*** The continuous and habitual nature and development of the action over the past, present and often future (She sings little by little and improves over time) Emphasizes progression and the gradual unfolding of a habitual action.\n\n* The basic meaning "go" applies to any kind of animate or inanimate motion: walk, ride, sail, fly, etc.\n\n* "voy a" can be pronounced /bwa/ and "me voy a" /mebwa/ or /mwa/.
  etymology: Inherited from Latin "īre" (“to go”). Forms beginning with /b/ such as voy (“I go”) are inherited from Latin "vādere" (“to go”). Forms beginning with /f/ such as fui (“I went”) are inherited from Latin "esse" (“to be”), which are a result of suppletion with ser (“to be”). The imperative voseo form, andá, is a result of suppletion with andar (“to walk”).
  gloss: to go (away from speaker and listener)
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to be suitable or apt for its purpose, to match or suit well (of clothing)
    q: said of things or people, intransitive
  gloss: to head towards or lead to a place away from the speaker
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to extend in time or space, from one point to another
    q: said of a thing, intransitive
  gloss: to play
    q: intransitive, card games
  gloss: to approach or consider (something) from a specific angle, or to direct attention towards a particular goal
    q: intransitive
  gloss: denotes an ongoing action with a sense of progression or movement through space or time; to go on (doing something), to carry on, to keep on
    id: progressive action
    q: preceding the gerund of a verb, intransitive
  gloss: an action or process is beginning to occur or unfold; to grow (dark), get (cloudy)
    q: preceding the gerund of a verb, intransitive
  gloss: used ironically with certain adjectives or passive participles
    q: colloquial, commonly paired with pues, intransitive
  gloss: used with the past participle of reflexive verbs to indicate being in a state caused by the verb
    id: auxiliary reflexive
    q: auxiliary
  gloss: whether something works or not
  gloss: to look, to be dressed
  gloss: to be about (a movie, a series, a game, a book, etc)
    syn: tratar
  gloss: to be going to; used to convey the near future tense [+a (+ infinitive) (do something)]
    q: auxiliary
  gloss: to be staked or bet in a dispute or competition (only used in the third-person plural present indicative form van to entice someone to engage in a bet)
    q: intransitive, said of a thing, especially a sum of money
  gloss: used with a hortative value to encourage or urge action; let's
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to habitually happen on a specific day or occasion; to habitually go
    q: intransitive, imperfective only
  gloss: used to describe the manner in which someone behaves or acts under specific conditions [+con (+ abstract noun)]
    q: intransitive
  gloss: indicates an inclination towards a particular profession or career path, especially one that is perceived in other people based on their current skills or personality
    q: intransitive
  gloss: indicates the pursuit of a particular career or vocation [+por (career or vocation)]
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to go fetch [+por (something) and a (somewhere)]
    q: intransitive
  gloss: indicates adherence to a specific linguistic pattern or rules, like conjugation
    q: intransitive, of an element of a language
  gloss: to come (towards or with the listener)
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to support a team [+dative]
    q: colloquial, intransitive
  gloss: to go away, to leave, to depart, to go (when the destination is not essential; when something or someone is going somewhere else)
    q: reflexive
    syn: andarse; marcharse
  gloss: to leak out (with liquids and gasses), to boil away, to go flat (gas in drinks)
    q: reflexive
  gloss: to overflow
    q: reflexive
  gloss: to go out (lights)
    q: reflexive
  gloss: to finish, to wear out, to disappear (e.g. money, paint, pains, mechanical parts)
    q: reflexive
  gloss: to die
    q: reflexive
  gloss: to break wind, to fart
    q: reflexive, informal
  gloss: to wet/soil oneself (i.e., urinate or defecate in one's pants)
    q: reflexive, informal
  gloss: to come, to cum, to ejaculate, to orgasm
    q: reflexive, vulgar
