  etymology: From Middle French "laid" (“hideous, ugly”), from Old French "laid, leid" (“unpleasant, horrible, odious”), from Vulgar Latin "*laitus" (“unpleasant, ugly”), from Frankish "*laiþ" (“unpleasant, obstinate, odious”), from Proto-Germanic "*laiþaz" (“sorrowful, unpleasant”), from Proto-Indo-European "*leyt-" (“unpleasant”). Akin to Old High German "leid" (“unpleasant, odious”) (German "leid" (“unfortunate”), Leid (“grief”)), Old Norse "leiþr" (“odious”), Old English "lāþ" (“unpleasant, odious”). More at loath.
