llegar
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}}
  usage: In nonstandard writing, there may be no g-gu alternation, so something like "llegué" may be written "llegé" (or even "llege"), but still pronounced "llegué".
  etymology: Inherited from Latin "applicāre" (“to land, to reach the coast”), due to the strong naval tradition in Iberian peninsula. Compare Portuguese "chegar", Asturian "allegar", also Romanian "pleca" (“to leave”), though the latter might be associated with folding up tents and leaving (see Latin plicāre).
  gloss: to arrive, get (to)
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to get up to, to go up to, to reach (+ a or + hasta)
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to be sufficient; to be enough
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to manage (be successful)
    q: intransitive
