llevar
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}}
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "levar", from Latin "levāre". The initial /ʎ/ developed from an earlier /lj/ in root-stressed conjugations such as lieva (< Latin lĕvat), where it resulted from the diphthongization of stressed Latin short ĕ to /je/. Eventually /ʎ-/ spread to the entire verb paradigm by analogy.
  gloss: to take somewhere
    q: transitive, ditransitive
    _gloss: to take away or take with oneself
      q: reflexive, transitive
  gloss: to carry, bear, hold
    q: transitive
  gloss: to get along (well, badly, etc.) [+nothing or (Latin America) la]
    q: reciprocal
  gloss: to wear
    q: transitive
    syn: llevar puesto; traer
  gloss: to have, include (have as a component, part, accessory or ingredient)
    q: transitive
  gloss: to give a lift, to give a ride
    q: transitive
  gloss: to have spent time, to have been [+direct object (length of time), along with locational phrase or gerund]
    q: transitive
  gloss: to take some amount of time [+dative (doer)]
  gloss: to have done, to have achieved a certain amount or extent of something (which is indicated by the verb and followed by a past participle)
    q: transitive
  gloss: to hold up, to be doing, to cope
    q: colloquial
  gloss: to wear
    q: reflexive
  gloss: to be in, to be fashionable
    q: reflexive, passive voice, informal
