  g: f
  etymology: Borrowed from Latin "līnea" (“line, thread”), from līnum (“flax”), from Proto-Indo-European "*līno-" (“flax”). Compare the inherited Old Spanish form liña, still used in archaic modern Spanish. Cognate with Portuguese "linha", Galician "liña", and Catalan "línia" and llinya.
  gloss: line (a straight path through two or more points)
    q: mathematics
  gloss: line (a piece of spoken dialogue in a script)
  gloss: line (a path for electrical current)
  gloss: telephone line (a physical telephone connection or a single telephony account)
  gloss: party line (an established policy that politicians should adhere to)
  gloss: line (similar directives in business and other contexts)
    q: figurative
  gloss: policy (the actual regulations and behavior of government agencies)
  gloss: line (a long thin pile of cocaine or other drug)
    q: slang
  gloss: linea, Spanish line (a traditional small unit of length, equivalent to 1.9 mm)
    q: historical
  gloss: queue (British), line (US)
    syn: fila
