meter
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}} {{es-conj|meterse|nocomb=1}}
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "meter", from Latin "mittere" (“to send”). The semantic shift from "send" to "put" probably occurred in Vulgar Latin.\n\nCognate with English "mess" and also mission, message. Compare also French "mettre", Friulian "meti", Portuguese "meter", and Italian "mettere".
  gloss: to put in, insert
    q: transitive
    syn: insertar; dentrotraer
  gloss: to score
    q: sports, transitive
  gloss: to make (noise)
  gloss: to cram, stuff, stick, shove, pack
    q: transitive
  gloss: to hit, to punch, to strike something hard
    q: ambitransitive, colloquial
    syn: pegar; dar
  gloss: to attempt something, to try hard
    q: colloquial
  gloss: to meddle, interfere, to get into
    q: reflexive
    syn: inmiscuirse; meter la nariz
  gloss: to mess with someone
    q: reflexive
  gloss: to get into (a small space)
    q: reflexive
  gloss: to get into, to get in
    id: meterse2
    q: reflexive
  gloss: to get into a profession
    q: reflexive, colloquial
  gloss: to do drugs
    q: reflexive, colloquial
