morir
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}}
  usage: * The reflexive form of this verb, morirse, is more informal, while the non-reflexive form, morir, is more formal.\n* Widely used figuratively, as in English:\n** Me morí del aburrimiento ― I died of boredom\n** Me morí del susto ― I died of fright
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "murir", morir, from Latin "morīrī", variant of morī. Cognate with Galician "morrer", Ladino "murir", and Portuguese "morrer". Compare English "mortify".
  gloss: to die
    q: intransitive, sometimes reflexive
    syn: estirar la pata; fallecer; morirse; palmar; irse; marchar; pasar; expirar; perecer; sucumbir; fenecer; finar; quedarse; apagarse; acabar; (colloquial) palmar; (colloquial) cascar; (colloquial) espichar; (colloquial) caer; (colloquial) sonar; (colloquial) petatearse; (colloquial) despichar; (colloquial) ñampiarse; (colloquial) jetearse; (colloquial) piantarse; (colloquial) pelarse; (vulgar, Venezuela) descoñetar; transir; transirse
  gloss: to be dying (for, to)
    q: intransitive, reflexive, idiomatic
  gloss: to end, to flow into something (a road, river, etc.)
    q: intransitive
  gloss: to kill
    q: passive voice
_____
