mío
pos: pron
  meta: {{head|es|pronoun|first person singular possessive of singular masculine||masculine plural|míos|feminine|mía|feminine plural|mías|g=m-s}}
  g: m-s
  usage: Typically preceded by a definite article (el mío, la mía, los míos, las mías) unless referring to clothing, body parts, or functioning as the copulative attribute of the verb ser, as in the above example. In these cases the use of the definite article indicates emphasis.
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "myo", from Latin "meus".
  gloss: mine; of mine
pos: determiner
  meta: {{head|es|determiner|first person singular possessive of singular masculine||masculine plural|míos|feminine|mía|feminine plural|mías|g=m-s}}
  g: m-s
  usage: * When used before a noun as part of the noun phrase, the apocopate forms mi and mis are used instead of the full form mío.\n:: Son mis libros. ― They are my books.\n:: Son los libros míos. ― They are my books / the books of mine\n* This pronoun is often used substantively, with the noun implied but omitted:\n:: ¿Es tu libro? Sí, es el mío. ― Is it your book? Yes, it is mine.\n* The usage of stressed possessive determiners and pronouns after place prepositions is common in colloquial Latin American speech but considered grammatically incorrect by the RAE.\n: : Apareció detrás mío. ― He/she/it appeared behind me.
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "myo", from Latin "meus".
  gloss: mine, my; of mine
    q: predicative or after the noun
