ni
pos: conj
  meta: {{head|es|conjunctions|cat2=coordinating conjunctions}}
  etymology: From Old Spanish "nin", from Latin "nec", apocopated form of neque, from Proto-Indo-European "*nekʷe" (“and not, neither, nor”), from *né (“not”) + *-kʷe (“and”). Compare Asturian and Galician "nin", Catalan and French "ni", Portuguese "nem", Italian "né", Dalmatian "ne". Indo-European cognates include Gothic "𐌽𐌹𐌷" and Irish "nach".
  gloss: Used when negating two or more elements, and not, not A or B, not A nor B, neither A nor B
pos: adv
  meta: {{es-adv}}
  etymology: From Old Spanish "nin", from Latin "nec", apocopated form of neque, from Proto-Indo-European "*nekʷe" (“and not, neither, nor”), from *né (“not”) + *-kʷe (“and”). Compare Asturian and Galician "nin", Catalan and French "ni", Portuguese "nem", Italian "né", Dalmatian "ne". Indo-European cognates include Gothic "𐌽𐌹𐌷" and Irish "nach".
  gloss: Used in emphatic negations, not even
    syn: ni siquiera
  gloss: Forms an emphatic negative imperative, don't even
    q: with the subjunctive
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|f|níes}}
  g: f
