noche
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|f}}
  g: f
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish noche, from Latin nox, from Proto-Italic &#42;nok⁽ʷ⁾ts, from Proto-Indo-European &#42;nókʷts.
  gloss: night (the period between sunset and sunrise)
  gloss: evening (after sunset)
    syn: tarde
pos: adv
  meta: {{es-adv}}
  usage: In Mexico and Central America, the adverb might accept degree adverbs (e.g. algo, muy, un poco, etc.), shifting its meaning to "late at night". The same phenomenon is observed in de noche (e.g. "Es muy de noche para estudiar." ("It's too late at night to study.")).
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish noche, from Latin nox, from Proto-Italic &#42;nok⁽ʷ⁾ts, from Proto-Indo-European &#42;nókʷts.
  gloss: after dark, (late) at night, nocturnally
    q: chiefly Mexico, Central America
    syn: de noche; por la noche
