olé
pos: interj
  meta: {{head|es|interjections}}
  usage: The interjection was commonly shouted in bullfighting every time the matador managed to avoid being hit by the bull. The sense was later brought to sports, mainly soccer, and is shouted when a player manages to dribble their opponent.
  etymology: Unknown. Often supposed to be from Arabic "الله" (“God!”), used e.g. to express surprise, excitement, etc., and/or from related وَاللهِ (“by God!”), used as an oath or strong agreement.\n\nThe Spanish Arabist Federico Corriente, however, counted the terms among the “falsos arabismos” (false Arabisms) in his work Diccionario de Arabismos y Voces Afines en Iberorromance.\n\nThe counterargument is mainly phonetic, الله being generally pronounced with a dark L and back vowels ([ɑlˤˈlˤɑːh]), which makes the Spanish /e/ problematic. Note, however, that the pronunciation becomes clear and front when /i/ precedes (e.g. لله [lɪlˈlæːh]). Perhaps this front articulation was generalized in (parts of) Andalusian Arabic.
  gloss: an expression of encouragement and approval
