    q: Andalusia, colloquial
pos: adv
  meta: {{head|es|adverb}}
  etymology: apocopic form of "pechada", matching the standard Andalusian phonetic reduction involving the loss of the intervocalic -d- in the suffix -ada.
  gloss: A lot, very much, hella
    q: Andalusia, colloquial
pos: determiner
  meta: {{head|es|determiner|g=f}}
  g: f
  usage: Originally a phonetic reduction of the feminine noun pechada (literally, "a chest-full"), traditionally used in the partitive construction una pechá de [noun]. In modern colloquial Andalusian speech—particularly in Malaga—it has undergone grammaticalization. By dropping both the indefinite article una and the preposition de, it now functions directly as an invariable determiner/quantifier placed immediately before a noun, completely independent of the noun's gender or number (e.g., pechá calor [masc. sg.], pechá perras [fem. pl.]).
  etymology: apocopic form of "pechada", matching the standard Andalusian phonetic reduction involving the loss of the intervocalic -d- in the suffix -ada.
  gloss: Much, a lot of, many
    q: Andalusia, Málaga, colloquial, invariable
