picado
pos: adj
  meta: {{es-adj}}
  usage: * In the colloquial senses of being annoyed, "salty," or competitive, this word functions as an adjective indicating a state of being, and is therefore almost exclusively used with the verb estar (e.g., estar picado). \n* It is the resulting state of the pronominal verb picarse (to get annoyed/salty, to take offense). \n* In colloquial speech, particularly in Andalusia and casual registers across Spain, the participle is frequently pronounced without the intervocalic "d" (e.g., estar picao).
  etymology: Past participle of picar. Equivalent to picar + -ado.
  gloss: salty, annoyed, resentful, or butthurt, especially after losing a game, an argument, or being teased
    q: colloquial
  gloss: fiercely competitive or hooked on beating someone; having a strong rivalry
    q: colloquial
  gloss: sour, spoiled, or having turned into vinegar
    q: of wine or food
  gloss: choppy, rough
    q: of the sea
