pluma
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|f}}
  g: f
  etymology: From Latin "plūma" (“feather”), taken as an early semi-learned term (Latin pl- normally becomes ll- in inherited Spanish), or it may have maintained a conservative pronunciation as it would have been in use by mainly the upper class. A popular evolution of the word may have once existed in pre-literary Spanish, as evidenced by the Old Spanish derivative llumazo (compare Portuguese "chumaço"; see also Spanish "chumacera", borrowed from a related Portuguese term). Cognate to English "plume".
  gloss: feather
  gloss: quill, quill pen
  gloss: pen, fountain pen
    syn: pluma estilográfica
  gloss: nib
    syn: plumín; plumilla
  gloss: ballpoint pen
    q: Mexico, US Spanish
    syn: bolígrafo
  gloss: writer, penman
