porque
pos: conj
  meta: {{head|es|conjunction}}
  usage: * por qué is used when asking questions ("why") and porque is used when giving answers ("because"). porqué is a noun derived from por qué which means "reason". Because por que can be a combination of the preposition por and the relative pronoun que, it can also be translated as "why", for example in "Esa es la razón por (la) que hice eso." ("That's the reason (why) I did that.") (literally: "That's the reason for which I did that" or "That's the reason due to which I did that").\n* The subordinate clause that porque introduces is most often in the indicative.\n* When porque is negated — when one thing is said not to be because of another — its subordinate clause can be in either the indicative or the subjunctive. Both "No lo hago porque te odie, lo hago por tu propio bien" and "No lo hago porque te odio, lo hago por tu propio bien" can be used, with a possible difference in meaning (the second sentence can mean that the person talking still hates the other person (even though it's unlikely based on context), while the first sentence can't), though the first sentence (with the subjunctive) is more common when the speaker doesnt hate the person being addressed.
  etymology: From por (“for; due to”) + que (“what”).
  gloss: because
    syn: car; ca
