  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "res", from the nominative of Latin "rēs" (“thing, property”). Cognate with an,ast,ca,gl "res" and Portuguese "rês".\n\nA derivation from Arabic "رَأْس" (“head”) is almost unanimously described in dictionaries and represents a perfect semantic match, but it fails on phonetic grounds: the front vowel of the Romance form speaks against an Arabic origin because dialects generally maintain a back -a- in this word (compare Maltese "ras", not *ries).
