pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m}}
  g: m
  etymology: Uncertain, probably borrowed from Italian "tacco"; ultimately from Proto-West Germanic "*takkō" (“twig, branch, shoot”).
  gloss: peg (a short, thick piece of wood, metal, or other material)
  gloss: dowel (a longer piece of wood, plastic, or other material)
    _gloss: cue (a stick used to play billiards, snooker, pool, etc)
      q: sports
      __gloss: billiards
        q: colloquial, Peru, Bolivia
      __gloss: billiards player
        q: slang, Venezuela, Ecuador
      __gloss: pool hall
        q: colloquial, Peru
    _gloss: polo-stick; mallet
      q: Rioplatense, sports
  gloss: stopper, plug, wad (small bundle of material made to cover, stop, or fill a hole)
  gloss: heel (of a shoe)
    q: Nicaragua, Puerto Rico, Andes, Dominican Republic, Cono Sur
    _gloss: cleats
      q: soccer, Mexico, Honduras, El Salvador, Nicaragua, Costa Rica, Panama, Colombia, Spain
      syn: tachones
    _gloss: shoe
      q: colloquial, Colombia
  gloss: traffic jam
    q: Chile
  gloss: curse word, swear word
    q: Spain
    syn: juramento; palabrota; voto
  gloss: a load, a lot
    q: Spain, colloquial
    syn: montón
  gloss: years of age
    q: Spain, colloquial, in the plural
    syn: año; primavera
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|m}}
  g: m
  etymology: Uncertain. One theory is that the original tacos were rolled (like what are now known as taquitos) and resembled the rolled-up explosive charges used in mines (tacos de minero), making it the same word as taco (“plug, wad”) above. Another theory is that it derives from Nahuatl "tlahco", referring to the placement of the filling; tortillas, though not tacos, are known to be pre-Columbian.
  gloss: taco
    q: Mexico, Central America, Bolivia, cooking
