terco
pos: adj
  meta: {{es-adj}}
  etymology: Attested from the fifteenth century, several farther etymologies have been suggested: \n\n* a shared proto-Romance word of cel origin, from Proto-Celtic "*terkos" (“scarce, meagre”), from Proto-Indo-European "*ters-" (“dry”), compare Irish "tearc" (“meagre”); \n* a derivation from Italian pirchio ("stingy") + tirato ("avaricious"); \n* or, reversing the usual derivation, from rare entercar (whence entercarse), syncopated from rare 16th. century *enternegar, from Latin "internecō" (“to slaughter”); or from Latin "tricae" (“trivia”), via a verb derived in Vulgar Latin. \n\nAs the word has no mediaeval attestation, a southern European borrowing from dialectal Italian may be most likely; of the proto-Romance theories, derivation from Latin "internecō" is phonetically the easiest. Probably cognate with Italian "tirchio" and Catalan "enterc" (“stiff, rigid”).
  gloss: stubborn, stiff-necked, obstinate, willful, dogged, pigheaded, hardheaded, bullheaded
    syn: obstinado; porfiado; testarudo
