tomar
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}}
  usage: In Latin America, beber tends to be used more with the meaning of "to drink alcohol", and tomar tends to be used instead for the normal "drink" sense. In Spain, both beber and tomar are common for the "drink" sense, with beber being the default verb. Beber is also commonly used in Spain in the "drink alcohol" sense.
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "tomar", of unknown origin.
  gloss: to take (put under one's physical or mental control, often in one's hands)
    q: chiefly  Latam  unless imperative
    syn: (chiefly Spain) coger
    _gloss: to draw from, take, derive
      q: figurative, both Latin America and Spain
      syn: sacar; (chiefly Spain) coger
  gloss: to grip, grasp, grab
    q: chiefly Latam
    syn: agarrar; sujetar; (chiefly Spain) coger
  gloss: to select, choose
  gloss: to seize, capture
    syn: capturar
  gloss: to take (a path, etc)
    syn: (chiefly Spain) coger
  gloss: to take (use as a means of transportation (when one isn't the driver))
    syn: (chiefly Spain) coger
  gloss: to take, consume, drink (mostly used with drinks and medication)
    q: colloquially chiefly  reflexive
    syn: (formal) ingerir; ("drink" sense) beber
    _gloss: used in tomar el sol
      q: figurative
  gloss: to take (regard in a specified way)
  gloss: to take (consider) [+por (for)]
    _gloss: to mistake for someone else
  gloss: to take, assume (a form, etc.), to turn
  gloss: to take a role or position
  gloss: to take (time)
    syn: llevar
  gloss: to take (a picture/photograph, etc.)
    q: transitive  (with foto, etc.) or ditransitive
    syn: (for pictures) sacar; hacer; echar
  gloss: to take (ascertain, find something out through some procedure)
  gloss: used in tomar notas/tomar apuntes/tomar nota/etc., to take (record) (notes/note, etc.)
  gloss: used in tomar asiento and the like, take (a seat)
  gloss: in other idiomatic expressions
