tuerca
pos: n
  meta: {{es-noun|f}}
  g: f
  etymology: According to the Coromines dictionary, probably a variation of older puerca (literally "female pig"), from Latin "porca", with a /t/ from tornillo (“bolt, screw”). The shift in meaning happened through comparison of bolts and nuts with pig's penises and female pigs, respectively. Compare other remodelings based on antonyms: Latin "sinexter", Latin "grevis", Latin "rendere". Compare similar semantic shifts in old and dialectal Italian "porchetta", Old French "escroue" (whence French "écrou", English "screw"), Dutch "schroef", German "Schraube" (both borrowed from some form of Latin "scrofa"), Navarro-Lapurdian aardi, ahardi and ardie ("nut" on a screw, but also "screw" and finally a wooden device with an adaptable hole on the center designed for the press), Aragonese "troyeta" ("tuerca o rosca de la prensa de vino" (please help translate)) (diminutive of trueya "female pig", cognate with Catalan "truja", French "truie", Italian "troia"). The original /p/ survives in Portuguese "porca" (“nut”), from Old Galician-Portuguese "porca". The Mexican "penis" sense is a minced oath of verga.
  gloss: nut (for a bolt)
    syn: matriz
  gloss: penis
    q: Mx, euphemistic
    syn: verga; Thesaurus:pene
_____
tuercebotas
pos: n
