  usage: In Spanish, if someone experiences bad luck, it can be said that a tuerto (“one-eyed person”) has seen them. So common expressions such as te ha mirado un tuerto or me miró un tuerto could be translated as "what rotten luck" or "I/He/She was jinxed".
  etymology: Inherited from Latin "tortus" (“twisted”).
  gloss: one-eyed person (someone blind in one eye)
  gloss: tort, injury, offense
  gloss: someone who is thought to bring bad luck to a person they see; evil eye
