un
pos: art
  meta: {{head|es|articles|g=m|indefinite||plural|unos|feminine|una|feminine plural|unas}}
  g: m
  usage: * When a feminine noun starts with a stressed a- or ha-, un is used instead of una to prevent the sound from being used twice.\n*: ¡Mira al cielo, hay un águila!\n*: ¡Manos arriba, tengo un arma!
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "un", from uno, from Latin "ūnus" (“one”), from itc-ola "oinos", from Proto-Italic "*oinos", from Proto-Indo-European "*h₁óynos" (“one”).
  gloss: an; a
pos: num
  meta: {{head|es|numerals|g=m|apocopate||standard form|uno}}
  g: m
  usage: The form un is only used before and within the noun phrase of the masculine singular noun that it modifies. In other positions, uno is used instead.
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "un", from uno, from Latin "ūnus" (“one”), from itc-ola "oinos", from Proto-Italic "*oinos", from Proto-Indo-European "*h₁óynos" (“one”).
  gloss: apocopic form of "uno" (“one”)
    q: before the noun
