ustedes
pos: pron
  meta: {{head|es|pronoun|g=mf-p|singular|usted|dative|les}}
  g: mf-p
  usage: Functionally, usted and ustedes are second person pronouns, but grammatically, the verbs they govern are conjugated in the third person (in Western Andalusia and the Canary Islands ustedes may also be used with verbs in the second person plural, but this is colloquial, nonstandard, and more common in rural areas, but overall not as common). See Appendix:Spanish pronouns for details.
  etymology: From usted.
  gloss: you (plural), you guys, y'all, yous, yinz, ye
    q: Latin America, US, Canary Islands, Western  Andalusia, Equatorial Guinea, Philippines, in Spain  formal
    syn: vosotros (the most common second-person plural pronoun in mainland Spain, Ceuta, Melilla and the Balearic Islands)
