venir
pos: v
  meta: {{es-verb}} {{es-conj}} {{es-conj|venirse|nocomb=1}}
  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish venir, from Latin veniō. Cognate with Ladino "venir".
  gloss: Senses relating to literal movement. [+de (from) or desde (from), along with a (to) or para (to, toward) or hacia (toward)]
    _gloss: to come (move closer to some location (most often closer to the speaker), by default the speaker's location)
      q: sometimes reflexive
      syn: (sometimes) ir
    _gloss: to arrive
      syn: llegar
  gloss: Senses relating to figurative movement
    _gloss: to come from, originate [+de (from)]
      syn: ser
    _gloss: to come (happen)
      syn: pasar; ocurrir
    _gloss: to come (appear)
      syn: aparecer
    _gloss: to come (to have some characteristic or quality) (with an adjective or prepositional phrase)
      syn: ir
    _gloss: to receive something, get something [+nominative (something) and dative (recipient)]
      syn: llegar
    _gloss: to get (something not very tangible, a feeling, a cold, a fever, a cough, pain, an urge, etc.) [+nominative (something) and dative (recipient)]
      syn: dar
    _gloss: to be coming or coming up (of a date, event, etc.)
      syn: llegar
    _gloss: translated with next (used with semana, mes, año, days of the week, etc.)
    _gloss: translated with be about, mean [+a]
    _gloss: translated with end up [+a and infinitive]
    _gloss: to be (with "bien" (convenient, helpful, good), "mal" (bad), etc.) [+nominative (something) and dative (for someone)]
    _gloss: to suit (well, badly) [+nominative (something) and dative (someone)]
      syn: ir; pegar
    _gloss: to fit, to be (of clothing, with adjectives like "grande" (be big), etc., or "bien" (fit well)) [+nominative (something) and dative (someone)]
      syn: quedar
    _gloss: to orgasm, to cum, to come
      q: reflexive, colloquial
