vosotros
pos: pron
  meta: {{head|es|pronoun|feminine|vosotras}}
  usage: The informal use of this pronoun, along with its oblique form os, is confined to the Philippines, Equatorial Guinea, and Spain (including all of Andalusia and speakers in the Canary Islands with relatives who speak mainland Spain dialects). Elsewhere in the Spanish-speaking world, it is found only in oratory, legal, and religious language. In western Andalusia and the Canary Islands, its associated verb forms may sometimes be used with ustedes (e.g. "ustedes estáis"). However, this is nonstandard, colloquial, and (in the case of Andalusia) more common in rural areas, but less common overall. Many speakers in the Canary Islands who by default use ustedes use both les and os and both conjugations ("están", and "estáis", but when the pronoun is present "ustedes están" is usually favored) due to influence from mainland Spain. In formal situations, or when speaking to speakers from mainland Spain, some Canarian speakers use vosotros and its conjugations instead of ustedes.
  etymology: From older vos (“you”) (plural), from Latin "vōs", and otros (“others”), plural of otro, from Latin alter (“other”). Compare Galician "vosoutros", Catalan "vosaltres", Occitan "vosautres", French "vous autres", Italian "voialtri", Sicilian "vuiautri".
  gloss: you, you guys; second person plural personal pronoun
    q: informal, mainland  Spain, Ceuta, Melilla, Balearic Islands, Equatorial Guinea, Philippines, archaic  in  Latin America
    syn: ustedes (the most common second-person plural pronoun in Latin America and the Canary Islands)
