  etymology: Inherited from Old Spanish "angel", a learned borrowing from Late Latin "angelus" (“messenger, angel”), or alternatively perhaps an early borrowing from an roa-ocr language (compare Catalan "àngel" and Occitan "àngel"), explaining the lack of a final /o/; however, a variant dialectal form ángelo is attested. The Latin word itself derives from Ancient Greek "ἄγγελος" (“messenger, envoy, angel”). Compare cognate Ladino "andjel" and the inherited Portuguese "anjo".
  gloss: angel (an incorporeal and sometimes divine messenger from a deity)
  gloss: angel (one of the lowest order of such beings, below virtues)
