‘ ’
pos: punct
  meta: {{head|es|punctuation mark|head=[[‘]] [[’]]|cat2=matched pairs}}
  usage: * The above quotation marks are typically called comillas simples.\n* An outer quotation is enclosed with <« »>, then within it an embedded (inner) quotation is typically enclosed with <“ ”>, then further within it <‘ ’> are used to enclose it.
  gloss: Encloses a word or expression that is being used in its conceptual meaning or as a definition of another
  gloss: Encloses an embedded (inner) quotation further inside an already embedded (inner) quotation
_____
“ ”
pos: punct
  meta: {{head|es|punctuation mark|head=[[“]] [[”]]|cat2=matched pairs}}
  usage: * The above quotation marks are typically called comillas altas or comillas inglesas.\n* An embedded (inner) quotation is enclosed with <“ ”>, within <« »> for the outer quotation.
  gloss: Encloses an embedded (inner) quotation
_____
℆
pos: symbol
  meta: {{head|es|symbol}}
  usage: * The ℆ symbol is used in Spanish item pricing—in a fashion similar to traditional use of the @ (“each priced at”) symbol in English.\n{|\n|+ Example:\n|-\n| apples ||align=right width=64px| @ €4\n|-\n| manzanas ||align=right| 4 € ℆\n|}\n\n* Note: the @ symbol itself—in Spanish and Portuguese—is the symbol for arroba; an old measure of weight and/or volume.
  etymology: Abbreviation of "cada uno" or cada una (“each one”).
  gloss: each one
